The checksums must DIFFER between first order and each higher-order arm: an identical one
would mean the correction was not applied and the row is measuring first order twice.

That guard does NOT extend to the Rhie-Chow arms. The checksum is a sum over millions of
values that is almost entirely cancellation, so a term can be present per node and invisible
in the total: at n=32 the scalar arm with scale 1.0 prints exactly the no-Rhie-Chow checksum
while scale 3.0 prints a different one, which is how that was established. Whether the term
reached the kernel is answered by ran_rc in the CSV and by the driver's per-node oracles
(--verify-ho --rhie-chow), never by these totals.
